  "content": {
    "id": "p22-q87-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "87",
    "prompt": "For $a,b>0$, show that\n$$\\left(\\frac1a\\right)^{\\ln(b)}=\\left(\\frac1b\\right)^{\\ln(a)}.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p22-q87-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Both sides have natural logarithm ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "-\\ln(a)\\ln(b)",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ", so they are equal.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p22-q87-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Take natural logarithms and use the power and reciprocal laws.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\ln\\left[\\left(\\frac1a\\right)^{\\ln(b)}\\right]&=\\ln(b)\\ln\\left(\\frac1a\\right)\\\\\\ln\\left[\\left(\\frac1a\\right)^{\\ln(b)}\\right]&=-\\ln(b)\\ln(a).\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}\\ln\\left[\\left(\\frac1b\\right)^{\\ln(a)}\\right]&=\\ln(a)\\ln\\left(\\frac1b\\right)\\\\\\ln\\left[\\left(\\frac1b\\right)^{\\ln(a)}\\right]&=-\\ln(a)\\ln(b).\\end{align*}$$\nThe logarithms are equal. Both original expressions are positive, and $\\ln$ is one-to-one, so\n$$\\left(\\frac1a\\right)^{\\ln(b)}=\\left(\\frac1b\\right)^{\\ln(a)}.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
